{
 "id": "l19",
 "label": "19",
 "title": "Magíng: to be or to become",
 "summary": "Using magíng before a noun or adjective to talk about changing from one state to another",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Understand what magíng adds before a noun or adjective",
  "Form the infinitive, imperative, past, present and future of magíng phrases",
  "Read and build sentences such as Gustó kong magíng gurò",
  "Describe how a person or thing is changing, has changed or will change"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What magíng means",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Magíng** means *to be* or *to become*. When it is placed **before a noun or an adjective**, the phrase expresses the speaker's or the subject's **desire to change from one state or condition to another**."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Magíng is in fact a **very common helping verb** in Tagalog. It does the job of English *to become*, and the noun or adjective after it says **what** someone or something becomes."
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Magíng + noun or adjective",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "+ adjective",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "magíng",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "mabaít",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "+ noun",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "magíng",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "gurò",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "to become good / to become a teacher"
    },
    {
